  "notes": "Duala, Bantu Sawabantu (A.24, Guthrie), lingua franca of the Duala and Mungo peoples of Cameroon's Littoral coast; first written 1870 by Alfred Saker (Baptist missionary Bible translation), with the modern General Alphabet of Cameroon Languages (AGLC, 1978) standardising it alongside other Cameroonian languages (Paulian 1971; Ittmann 1939). Seven-vowel system /i e ɛ a ɔ o u/, no length contrast recorded in the citing sources. Prenasalised stops/affricates ⟨mb mp nd ndj nt ŋg ŋk⟩ are single segments, distinguishing the voiceless series (⟨mp nt ŋk⟩) from the voiced (⟨mb nd ŋg⟩), plus the implosives ⟨ɓ ɗ⟩ distinct from plain ⟨b d⟩. Duala is tonal but tone is often left unmarked in ordinary writing; where marked, high/rising takes the acute and low/mid is unmarked (omniglot; Ittmann 1939).",
